Z||000002||mshill||04-24-2000||06:25 PM||||Don't mess with the clutch master or slave cylinder rods.  You will end up destroying your new clutch and or throwout bearing if you ever get things working properly.<P>First, search this site for threads on bleeding the clutch, bent clutch pedals, and clutch problems in general.  There are tons of archived threads on how to do this correctly.  Get a Haynes manual and go through the shift linkage adjustment procedure in it.  Once you verify that the master and slave cylinders are not leaking, bleed the system properly.  Then if you still have problems do the shift linkage adjustment.  If you still have problems check for the bent clutch pedal problem (again search the forum for threads on this).  If you still have problems, do it all again, becuase the system will work perfectly if you have no leaks, no air in the lines and the linkage is adjusted properly.  I have been there.<P>As for the timing cover leak, did you replace the main shaft seal?  Use proper gaskets/RTV to seal up the timing cover?  At 2 qts per day, you don't have a leak, that's a BIG HOLE.  Find the source of the leak and figure out what went wrong.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by mshill (edited 04-24-2000).]||||reg||1
